.\" Manual page for the trusted CA file pam_ssoossh reads.
.TH PAM_SSOOSSH\-CA 5 "2026-09-05" "pam_ssoossh" "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
pam_ssoossh\-ca \- trusted certificate authorities for pam_ssoossh
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/ssoossh/ca.pub
.SH DESCRIPTION
The file named by
.BR pam_ssoossh (8)'s
.B trusted\-ca\-file
argument lists the certificate authorities whose signature on a certificate
is accepted. It is the
.BR authorized_keys (5)
public\-key format: one key per line, as
.I type base64 [comment]
\(em the same line
.BR ssh\-keygen (1)
writes to a
.I .pub
file and
.BR sshd (8)
reads from
.BR TrustedUserCAKeys .
The public half of the CA
.B ssoosshd
signs with is what belongs here.
.PP
Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored. Options before the key type, as
.BR sshd (8)
allows in
.BR authorized_keys ,
are not: a line must start with the key type.
.SH ROTATION
The file is read on every authentication, so a CA can be added before the
server starts signing with it and the old one removed afterwards, with no
restart of anything on the host. A file may list several CAs at once and a
certificate signed by any of them passes.
.PP
A key of a type this build's crypto cannot verify (see
.B CERTIFICATE ALGORITHMS
in
.BR pam_ssoossh (8))
is skipped with a warning naming its type and line number, and the rest of
the file still applies. That is deliberate: an Ed25519 CA appended on a
Friday must not take every host of a different platform offline, and must
certainly not do it with "not signed by a trusted CA" as the only
explanation. A file with no usable key at all, or that cannot be read, makes
.BR pam_ssoossh (8)
return
.B PAM_NO_MODULE_DATA
before it contacts the server.
.PP
A line that is present but not a key \(em a truncated paste, a base64 typo
\(em is a malformed file, and the whole file is refused with an error naming
the line. Keys that parsed are not used from a file that also holds
garbage, because a file in that state is more likely mid\-edit than
intended.
.SH LIMITS
Up to 32 keys are read; further lines are ignored with a warning. A file
larger than 32 KiB is refused, and a line longer than 3072 bytes is
malformed. Real CA files are a few hundred bytes.
.SH EXAMPLE
.RS
.nf
# CAs ssoosshd signs PAM certificates with. Rotation in progress:
# the ECDSA key is current, the Ed25519 one takes over next week.
ecdsa\-sha2\-nistp384 AAAAE2VjZHNhLXNoYTItbmlzdHAzODQAAAAIbmlzdHAzODQAAABh... ssoossh\-ca\-2026
ssh\-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIJDgdf6ZrlMZNcQIVtB0PPlHZxqh/cv1NWNFbfays7RF ssoossh\-ca\-2027
.fi
.RE
.PP
To check what a host will accept, the fingerprints
.BR ssh\-keygen (1)
prints are the ones the module logs:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ssh\-keygen \-l \-f /etc/ssoossh/ca.pub
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.I /etc/ssoossh/ca.pub
is the path the shipped examples use. Nothing in the module fixes it; the
file should be owned by root and readable by the users of the services
that load the module, which for
.BR sudo (8)
and
.BR sshd (8)
means root alone.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pam_ssoossh (8),
.BR pam_ssoossh\-principals (5),
.BR ssh\-keygen (1),
.BR sshd (8)
